---
source: crates/agmem-server/tests/protocol.rs
expression: "&tools.tools"
---
[
  {
    "name": "consolidate",
    "description": "Find what stored memory needs tidying up, and get back everything needed to do it. This decides nothing and changes nothing.\n\nCall it when memory has started to feel noisy: recall keeps returning the same claim in different words, two stored claims look like they disagree, or you are picking up a project that has accumulated a lot. It is a maintenance verb, not a search one — to find a particular claim, use `recall`.\n\nThree lists come back, and every claim in each carries its full text rather than only its id, so you can judge it here instead of looking it up:\n\n- `near_duplicates` — groups of live claims saying the same thing. Merge a group with one `remember` call: the one wording worth keeping, and `supersedes` set to the ids of every other member. `supersedes` takes a list, so a group of any size closes in that single call — reaching for `forget` instead deletes the history the merge exists to keep. Read `min_similarity` before you do: it is the weakest pair anywhere in the group, not the weakest link, so a low number means the group chained together through a middle claim and may not be one claim at all.\n- `contradictions` — pairs about the same subject that are close without being the same. Nothing here has judged that they disagree; read both and decide. When one of them is wrong, send the right one with the wrong one's id in `supersedes`.\n- `stale_contexts` — claims filed as short-lived that recall has kept alive far past the point their class would have expired them. If one turned out to be durable, store it again with a slower `decay_class`; if it was only scaffolding for one session, `forget` it.\n\nEvery list can be empty, and usually some are — an empty answer means there is nothing worth your attention, not that the call failed. `scanned` says what was actually compared.\n\nThis looks in the current space only, unlike every other read here: a tidy-up should not reach the shared user space unless you name it with `space`.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to look: `current`, `user`, `all`, or a space name. Defaults to\n`current` alone — unlike every other read, which also covers `user`.\nMaintenance is scoped to the project you are in unless you widen it.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "Cluster": {
          "description": "Live claims close enough to be one claim.",
          "properties": {
            "max_similarity": {
              "description": "The closest pair in the cluster.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "members": {
              "description": "The claims, strongest first — the first is the natural survivor, and\neach carries its own text so the merge can be judged rather than\nguessed.",
              "items": {
                "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "min_similarity": {
              "description": "The **weakest** similarity between any two members, not merely between\nthe two that were linked. A cluster forms transitively, so a low number\nhere means it chained: A resembles B and B resembles C, while A and C\nmay be about different things. Read it before merging the whole group.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space they are in. Ids are only meaningful inside one.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "space",
            "members",
            "min_similarity",
            "max_similarity"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "Contradiction": {
          "description": "Two live claims naming one subject, offered so the caller can decide which\nof them is true.",
          "properties": {
            "a": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView",
              "description": "The stronger claim, with its text."
            },
            "b": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView",
              "description": "The other one, with its text."
            },
            "shared_entities": {
              "description": "The subjects they both name — what makes them comparable at all.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "similarity": {
              "description": "How close they are. High is not evidence of agreement: measured with\nthis embedder, real contradictions score 0.92–0.97 — above the\nclustering bar — because the vector carries the subject, not the\npolarity. Read both claims.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space they are in.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "space",
            "shared_entities",
            "a",
            "b",
            "similarity"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "DecayClass": {
          "description": "How fast a memory's retention falls off between accesses.\n\nThe rates themselves live with the scoring functions; this is the label\nstored on the row.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "pinned",
              "description": "Never decays.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "slow",
              "description": "Fades over months.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "normal",
              "description": "The default: fades over weeks.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "fast",
              "description": "Working context: fades over days, then gets pruned at startup.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "Kind": {
          "description": "What a memory *is*, which decides how it is used and how fast it fades.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule, always part of assembled context.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "MemoryView": {
          "description": "A memory as `inspect` renders it: the whole row, including the counters\n`recall` folds away into a score.",
          "properties": {
            "access_count": {
              "description": "How often recall has returned it.",
              "format": "uint32",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "content": {
              "description": "The claim itself.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "created_at": {
              "description": "When the row was written, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "decay_class": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/DecayClass",
              "description": "How fast it fades between uses."
            },
            "derived_from": {
              "description": "What this claim was reflected out of, when `reflect` wrote it: the\nmemories and episodes it was drawn from, as refs to hand straight back\nto `inspect`. Absent on a claim that cites nothing.\n\nThis is what makes a conclusion checkable rather than something to take\non faith — the evidence is named, and each piece of it has its own\nhistory to walk.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": [
                "array",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "entities": {
              "description": "The subjects it is about.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The record id.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "invalid_at": {
              "description": "When it stopped; absent while it is still live.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "invalid_reason": {
              "description": "Why it stopped: `superseded`, `forgotten`, or `expired`.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "kind": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind",
              "description": "What the claim is."
            },
            "last_accessed": {
              "description": "When recall last returned it, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "source": {
              "description": "Where it came from: `agent`, `episode:<id>`, or `external:<origin>`.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space holding it.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "strength": {
              "description": "Ebbinghaus stability: 1.0 when written, raised by every recall that\nreturns it. A high number on an old memory means it is still in use.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "superseded_by": {
              "description": "The claim that corrected this one.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "supersedes": {
              "description": "The claims this one corrected — several when it merged a duplicate\ncluster into one wording. Absent, not empty, when it corrected nothing\n(see the `derived_from` note above on why this is `Option`).",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": [
                "array",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "tags": {
              "description": "Its labels.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "valid_from": {
              "description": "When the claim started being true, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "kind",
            "content",
            "space",
            "entities",
            "tags",
            "source",
            "decay_class",
            "strength",
            "access_count",
            "last_accessed",
            "valid_from",
            "created_at"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "SpaceScan": {
          "description": "How much of one space this pass actually looked at.",
          "properties": {
            "compared": {
              "description": "How many live memories carried a vector and were compared against each\nother. Zero means either an empty space or BM25-only mode — `note`\nsays which.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "truncated": {
              "description": "Whether the space holds more live memories than one pass compares. The\nstrongest were kept; run again after acting on these.",
              "type": "boolean"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "space",
            "compared",
            "truncated"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "StaleContext": {
          "description": "A short-lived note that outlived its class.",
          "properties": {
            "claim": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView",
              "description": "The claim, with its text and its counters."
            },
            "expires_in_days": {
              "description": "How much longer the automatic sweep will leave it, because every recall\nextended its horizon. A large number is the finding: this stopped being\na short-lived note some time ago.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "idle_days": {
              "description": "How long since it was last recalled.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "claim",
            "idle_days",
            "expires_in_days"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "contradictions": {
          "description": "Pairs of live claims about one subject that may disagree. Nothing here\njudges that they do — read both and decide, and expect to find some of\nthese in `near_duplicates` too: an embedding cannot tell a claim from\nits own negation, so the two lists overlap by design.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Contradiction"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "near_duplicates": {
          "description": "Groups of live claims saying the same thing. Merge one by sending\n`remember` with the surviving wording and `supersedes` set to the ids\nof every other member — one call, one live claim, and each closed\nmember still readable and pointing at what replaced it. Do not reach\nfor `forget` here: that deletes the correction history a merge exists\nto keep.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Cluster"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "note": {
          "description": "Present only when something limited the answer — no embedder, or a\nspace larger than one pass compares.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "scanned": {
          "description": "What each space held, so an empty answer can be told apart from an\nunexamined one.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/SpaceScan"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "spaces": {
          "description": "The spaces that were examined.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "stale_contexts": {
          "description": "Short-lived notes that recall has kept alive past the point their class\nwould have expired them.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/StaleContext"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "spaces",
        "scanned",
        "near_duplicates",
        "contradictions",
        "stale_contexts"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "openWorldHint": false
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "context",
    "description": "Read this before your first move in a session: the standing instructions, who the user is, what is relevant right now, and the lessons earlier sessions paid for — as one markdown block.\n\nCall it once at the start of a session, and again when the topic shifts enough that a different set of memories matters. Pass `query` to aim the Relevant section at what you are about to do; leave it out for a general orientation. The other sections do not change with it.\n\nThe block is capped at `budget_chars` (6000 by default) and fills its sections in priority order, dropping whole entries rather than cutting one in half — so it is a briefing, not an inventory. Use `recall` for anything it left out, including the verbatim text, which never appears here.\n\nEvery line ends with its memory id. Hand that to `inspect` to see where the claim came from, or to `remember`'s `supersedes` the moment you learn it is wrong.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "budget_chars": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "How many characters the block may take, 6000 by default. Sections fill\nin priority order until it runs out; whole entries are dropped, never\ncut mid-sentence.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": [
            "integer",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "query": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "What this session is about, in words. It aims the Relevant section at\nthe work in front of you; the other three sections do not change with\nit. Leave it out for a general orientation.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to look: `current` for this project, `user` for the person,\n`all` for every space, or a space name. Defaults to `current` and\n`user` together.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "openWorldHint": false
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "forget",
    "description": "Remove memories the store should no longer act on — by id when you know which, or by query when you do not.\n\nReach for `remember` with `supersedes` first: a claim that turned out to be wrong is a correction, and a correction stays readable and dated. Forget is for what should not have been stored at all — something private, something that only made sense inside one session, notes on a project that no longer exists.\n\nBy default a forgotten memory is closed, not deleted: it stops surfacing in `recall` and `context`, and stays visible to `inspect` with `forgotten` as the reason, so a mistaken forget is recoverable and an audit still adds up. `purge: true` deletes outright — the claim, its whole correction history, and for an episode its verbatim text and slices. That is unrecoverable, and it is the only way to remove something that must not stay on disk. Purging text does not purge the claims distilled from it.\n\nForgetting by `query` takes two calls: send it once with `dry_run: true`, read exactly what matched, then send the identical call with `dry_run: false` to act. Any other second call is refused — including the same query with `purge` flipped. Ids need no dry run, though `dry_run: true` previews them too.\n\nA query here matches on the words you write, not on their meaning: it selects the memories that contain those terms, so write the words you want gone. That is the opposite of `recall`, and deliberately — a deletion should never reach something that merely resembles what you asked for.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "dry_run": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Report what would be forgotten and change nothing. Required as the\nfirst of two calls when forgetting by `query`.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "ids": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "The ids to forget: `memory:<id>`, `episode:<id>`, or a bare id from a\n`recall` hit. Exact, and needs no dry run.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "purge": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Delete outright instead of closing. Unrecoverable: it takes the\nclaim's whole correction history with it, and an episode's verbatim\ntext and slices. Off by default.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "query": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "What to forget, in words, when you do not have the ids. Matched on the\nwords themselves, not on meaning — so it selects what you wrote rather\nthan what resembles it. Refused unless the identical call has already\nbeen made with `dry_run: true`.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to look: `current`, `user`, `all`, or a space name. Defaults to\n`current` and `user` together.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "ForgetMatch": {
          "description": "One row a forget selected.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "The claim, or the opening of the verbatim text, cut at 300 characters.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "derived": {
              "description": "For an episode: how many distilled claims cite it and will outlive it.\nPurging text does not purge what was learned from it.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": [
                "integer",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "Its id. Pass it to `inspect` to read the whole row before deciding.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "invalid_reason": {
              "description": "Why it is already closed, when it is — `superseded`, `forgotten`, or\n`expired`. A soft forget leaves such a row exactly as it is, so this is\nwhat explains an id in `matched` that is missing from `invalidated`.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "kind": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/MatchKind",
              "description": "What it is."
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space holding it.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "kind",
            "content",
            "space"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "MatchKind": {
          "description": "What a match is.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "memory",
              "description": "A distilled claim.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "episode",
              "description": "Verbatim text.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "chunks_purged": {
          "description": "How many episode slices went with the purged episodes.",
          "format": "uint",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "dry_run": {
          "description": "Whether this was the scope check rather than the act.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "invalidated": {
          "description": "The memories closed by this call. Shorter than `matched` when\nsomething in it was already closed.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "matched": {
          "description": "Everything the request selected, whether or not it moved. A purge\nlists the correction history it pulls in, which is the blast radius\nthe dry run exists to show.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/ForgetMatch"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "purge": {
          "description": "Whether the rows were deleted rather than closed.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "purged": {
          "description": "The memories and episodes deleted by this call.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "spaces": {
          "description": "The spaces this call looked in.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "spaces",
        "dry_run",
        "purge",
        "matched",
        "invalidated",
        "purged",
        "chunks_purged"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "destructiveHint": true
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "inspect",
    "description": "Look behind a stored memory: where it came from, what it used to say, and what the store actually holds.\n\nUse it when a recalled claim matters enough to check, when two claims disagree, or when you need to quote the original wording rather than the distilled version. `ref` takes one of: a memory id (bare, or `memory:<id>`) for the claim, its full correction history oldest-first, and the verbatim text it was distilled from; `episode:<id>` for that text with every claim drawn from it; `entity:<name>` for everything ever said about a subject, corrected claims included; or `stats` for per-space counts.\n\nNothing is ever deleted here, so a claim that was corrected is still readable and still dated — which is what lets you tell a belief that changed from a belief that was always wrong.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "ref": {
          "description": "What to look at: `memory:<id>` for a claim's history and the text\nbehind it, `episode:<id>` for stored verbatim text, `entity:<name>`\nfor everything said about a subject, or `stats` for what each space\nholds. Any bare id another call handed you also works, whichever of\nthose it turns out to name.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to look: `current`, `user`, `all`, or a space name. Defaults to\n`current` and `user` together — or to `all` for `stats`, which is a\nquestion about the whole store.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "ref"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "ChunkView": {
          "description": "One retrieval slice of an episode.",
          "properties": {
            "id": {
              "description": "The record id.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "position": {
              "description": "Zero-based position within the episode.",
              "format": "uint32",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "text": {
              "description": "The slice.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "position",
            "text"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "DecayClass": {
          "description": "How fast a memory's retention falls off between accesses.\n\nThe rates themselves live with the scoring functions; this is the label\nstored on the row.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "pinned",
              "description": "Never decays.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "slow",
              "description": "Fades over months.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "normal",
              "description": "The default: fades over weeks.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "fast",
              "description": "Working context: fades over days, then gets pruned at startup.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "EpisodeView": {
          "description": "Verbatim text as stored — never rewritten, never superseded.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "The text, exactly as it was given.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "created_at": {
              "description": "When the row was written, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The record id.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "occurred_at": {
              "description": "When the events described happened, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "session": {
              "description": "The conversation or working session it belongs to.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space holding it.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "space",
            "content",
            "occurred_at",
            "created_at"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "Inspected": {
          "description": "One of the four answers, tagged by `kind`.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "description": "A claim, where it came from, and what it used to say.",
              "properties": {
                "chain": {
                  "description": "Its whole supersession chain, oldest first, this claim included. A\nclaim that has never been corrected is a chain of one; a longer one\nis a belief that changed, and each link is dated.",
                  "items": {
                    "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView"
                  },
                  "type": "array"
                },
                "episode": {
                  "anyOf": [
                    {
                      "$ref": "#/$defs/EpisodeView"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "null"
                    }
                  ],
                  "description": "The verbatim text it was distilled from, when there is one. Quote\nfrom this rather than from the claim when precision matters."
                },
                "kind": {
                  "const": "memory",
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "memory": {
                  "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView",
                  "description": "The claim asked about."
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "kind",
                "memory",
                "chain"
              ],
              "type": "object"
            },
            {
              "description": "Stored verbatim text and everything that came out of it.",
              "properties": {
                "chunks": {
                  "description": "The slices retrieval matches against, in reading order.",
                  "items": {
                    "$ref": "#/$defs/ChunkView"
                  },
                  "type": "array"
                },
                "derived": {
                  "description": "The claims distilled from it, oldest first.",
                  "items": {
                    "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView"
                  },
                  "type": "array"
                },
                "episode": {
                  "$ref": "#/$defs/EpisodeView",
                  "description": "The text, unedited."
                },
                "kind": {
                  "const": "episode",
                  "type": "string"
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "kind",
                "episode",
                "chunks",
                "derived"
              ],
              "type": "object"
            },
            {
              "description": "Everything said about one subject.",
              "properties": {
                "entity": {
                  "description": "The subject asked about.",
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "kind": {
                  "const": "entity",
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "memories": {
                  "description": "Every claim naming it, strongest first — closed ones included, so\na contradiction shows up as history rather than as a conflict.",
                  "items": {
                    "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryView"
                  },
                  "type": "array"
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "kind",
                "entity",
                "memories"
              ],
              "type": "object"
            },
            {
              "description": "What the store holds.",
              "properties": {
                "counts": {
                  "description": "One entry per space searched.",
                  "items": {
                    "$ref": "#/$defs/SpaceCounts"
                  },
                  "type": "array"
                },
                "kind": {
                  "const": "stats",
                  "type": "string"
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "kind",
                "counts"
              ],
              "type": "object"
            }
          ]
        },
        "Kind": {
          "description": "What a memory *is*, which decides how it is used and how fast it fades.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule, always part of assembled context.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "KindCount": {
          "description": "How many live memories of one kind a space holds.",
          "properties": {
            "count": {
              "description": "How many.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "kind": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind",
              "description": "The kind."
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "kind",
            "count"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "MemoryView": {
          "description": "A memory as `inspect` renders it: the whole row, including the counters\n`recall` folds away into a score.",
          "properties": {
            "access_count": {
              "description": "How often recall has returned it.",
              "format": "uint32",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "content": {
              "description": "The claim itself.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "created_at": {
              "description": "When the row was written, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "decay_class": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/DecayClass",
              "description": "How fast it fades between uses."
            },
            "derived_from": {
              "description": "What this claim was reflected out of, when `reflect` wrote it: the\nmemories and episodes it was drawn from, as refs to hand straight back\nto `inspect`. Absent on a claim that cites nothing.\n\nThis is what makes a conclusion checkable rather than something to take\non faith — the evidence is named, and each piece of it has its own\nhistory to walk.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": [
                "array",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "entities": {
              "description": "The subjects it is about.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The record id.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "invalid_at": {
              "description": "When it stopped; absent while it is still live.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "invalid_reason": {
              "description": "Why it stopped: `superseded`, `forgotten`, or `expired`.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "kind": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind",
              "description": "What the claim is."
            },
            "last_accessed": {
              "description": "When recall last returned it, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "source": {
              "description": "Where it came from: `agent`, `episode:<id>`, or `external:<origin>`.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space holding it.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "strength": {
              "description": "Ebbinghaus stability: 1.0 when written, raised by every recall that\nreturns it. A high number on an old memory means it is still in use.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "superseded_by": {
              "description": "The claim that corrected this one.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "supersedes": {
              "description": "The claims this one corrected — several when it merged a duplicate\ncluster into one wording. Absent, not empty, when it corrected nothing\n(see the `derived_from` note above on why this is `Option`).",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": [
                "array",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "tags": {
              "description": "Its labels.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "valid_from": {
              "description": "When the claim started being true, RFC3339.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "kind",
            "content",
            "space",
            "entities",
            "tags",
            "source",
            "decay_class",
            "strength",
            "access_count",
            "last_accessed",
            "valid_from",
            "created_at"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "SpaceCounts": {
          "description": "What one space holds.",
          "properties": {
            "chunks": {
              "description": "Retrieval slices of those episodes.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "episodes": {
              "description": "Verbatim episodes.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "invalidated": {
              "description": "Those closed — superseded, forgotten, or expired. Memories are never\ndeleted, so this is history rather than loss.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "live": {
              "description": "Those still live.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "live_by_kind": {
              "description": "Live memories per kind; a kind with none is absent.",
              "items": {
                "$ref": "#/$defs/KindCount"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "memories": {
              "description": "Every memory ever written here, live or closed.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space these counts describe.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "space",
            "memories",
            "live",
            "invalidated",
            "episodes",
            "chunks",
            "live_by_kind"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "found": {
          "$ref": "#/$defs/Inspected",
          "description": "The answer, shaped by what `ref` named."
        },
        "ref": {
          "description": "The reference this answers, in its canonical form.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "spaces": {
          "description": "The spaces that were searched.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "ref",
        "spaces",
        "found"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "openWorldHint": false
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "recall",
    "description": "Search everything past sessions stored — distilled claims and the verbatim text behind them — before assuming, guessing, or asking the user something they may already have said.\n\nCall this at the start of a session, when a new topic comes up, and before any answer that depends on what the user prefers, decided earlier, or has already been told. Ask in words: the wording is matched literally and the meaning semantically, so a question works better than keywords. Drop `query` entirely to list what `entities`, `tags` or `kinds` select on their own.\n\nHits come back ranked by how well they matched, how well they have held up since they were last used, and how important the storing agent said they were — each of those is in `signals`, so a claim that only surfaced because it never decays is visible as such. Every hit also carries the `source` it was distilled from, which `inspect` takes as it stands — a claim worth acting on is one to check, not one to hedge around. Nothing is hidden: a claim that was corrected is absent unless you ask with `as_of` or `include_invalidated`, which is how you find out what was believed at some earlier point.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "Kind": {
          "description": "What a memory *is*, which decides how it is used and how fast it fades.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule, always part of assembled context.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "as_of": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "What was believed at this instant, RFC3339 — corrections are dated, so\nthis returns the claim that was live then rather than the one that\nreplaced it.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "entities": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "Keep only memories about one of these subjects.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "include_invalidated": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Include claims that have since been corrected or forgotten. Off by\ndefault: a closed claim is history, not an answer.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "k": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "How many hits to return. Defaults to 10.",
          "format": "uint16",
          "maximum": 65535,
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": [
            "integer",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "kinds": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "Keep only these kinds.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "query": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "What you want to know, in words — a question or a topic, not keywords.\nBoth halves of retrieval use it: the wording matches literally, the\nmeaning matches semantically. Leave it out to list what the filters\nalone select, strongest first.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to look: `current` for this project, `user` for the person,\n`all` for every space, or a space name. Defaults to `current` and\n`user` together, which is almost always what you want.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "tags": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "Keep only memories carrying one of these labels.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "HitKind": {
          "description": "What a hit is.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "episode",
              "description": "A slice of verbatim text, stored unedited as ground truth.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "HitSignals": {
          "description": "The signals behind one hit's `score`, all in `[0, 1]` except `rrf`.",
          "properties": {
            "importance": {
              "description": "The standing importance of its decay class — what keeps a rarely\nmatched instruction ahead of a well matched scratch note.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "retention": {
              "description": "How much of the memory has survived its decay curve since it was last\nused. 1.0 for a pinned memory, or for verbatim text.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "rrf": {
              "description": "How well retrieval matched, fused across the text and vector arms. It\nhas no fixed scale; compare `rrf_normalized` instead.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "rrf_normalized": {
              "description": "`rrf` on a 0–1 scale against the rest of this call: 1.0 for the\nstrongest retrieval hit, 0.0 for the weakest one returned — or 0.0\neverywhere when nothing matched on words or meaning at all.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "rrf",
            "rrf_normalized",
            "retention",
            "importance"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "RecallHit": {
          "description": "One match, with the reasoning behind its place in the order.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "The claim, or the verbatim slice.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "entities": {
              "description": "The subjects this claim is about.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The id of this hit. Pass it to `inspect` to see where it came from, or\nto `remember`'s `supersedes` to correct it — the latter only for a claim,\nsince an `episode` hit is a slice of verbatim text and nothing to\ncorrect.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "invalid_at": {
              "description": "When it stopped being true; absent while it is still live.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "invalid_reason": {
              "description": "Why it stopped: `superseded`, `forgotten`, or `expired`.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "kind": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/HitKind",
              "description": "What this is: a distilled claim, or `episode` for a slice of the\nverbatim text one was distilled from."
            },
            "score": {
              "description": "The final rank, combining everything in `signals`. Comparable within\none call and not across calls.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            },
            "signals": {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/HitSignals",
              "description": "Why it surfaced."
            },
            "source": {
              "description": "Where it came from: `agent`, `episode:<id>`, or `external:<origin>`.\nPass it to `inspect` to read the text behind a distilled claim.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "space": {
              "description": "The space it came from.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "superseded_by": {
              "description": "The id of the claim that replaced this one.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "tags": {
              "description": "Its labels.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "valid_from": {
              "description": "When the claim started being true, RFC3339.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "kind",
            "content",
            "space",
            "score",
            "signals",
            "source",
            "entities",
            "tags"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "Truncated": {
          "description": "What a `k` left behind.\n\nA page of hits looks exactly like a whole store: fifty out of fifty and\nfifty out of five hundred serialise identically, so an agent that reads a\npage and reports what memory holds is right by luck. This says which of\nthe two happened, in the answer, at the moment it matters.",
          "properties": {
            "k": {
              "description": "The `k` that did the cutting.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "matching_claims": {
              "description": "How many live claims the filters select across the searched spaces,\nignoring `query` and `k`. Relevance is not counted here: this is the\nsize of the set the ranking chose from, not of what matched well.",
              "format": "uint64",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "note": {
              "description": "The same thing in words, because a number is only acted on when\nsomething says why it matters.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "returned_claims": {
              "description": "How many of them this answer carries.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "matching_claims",
            "returned_claims",
            "k",
            "note"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "hits": {
          "description": "The matches, highest `score` first.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/RecallHit"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "spaces": {
          "description": "The spaces this call actually searched.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "truncated": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/Truncated"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Present when this answer filled `k` and the filters select more claims\nthan it carries — so what came back is a page. Absent means `k` did not\ncut anything short: either the page never filled, or nothing beyond it\nmatches these filters."
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "spaces",
        "hits"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "openWorldHint": false
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "reflect",
    "description": "Store something you worked out from what memory already holds, together with the ids it was drawn from.\n\nCall it when reading several memories together tells you something none of them says on its own: a pattern across three failures, what a preference and a constraint mean taken together, the reason behind a decision that only became visible later. This is the verb for a conclusion you reached; `remember` is the verb for something you were told.\n\n`derived_from` is required, and it is the point. Pass the ids you actually read — memory ids, episode ids, bare or prefixed, exactly as `recall`, `remember`, `context` or `inspect` handed them to you. They are stored on the insight and shown by `inspect`, so a later session can see what the conclusion was built on and check whether that evidence still stands, instead of taking it on faith. An insight with nothing behind it is a `remember` call.\n\nStored as a `lesson` unless you say otherwise, which fades slowly and appears in the Lessons section of `context`. Ids are looked for in this space and in the shared `user` space, so an insight about the project may cite what is known about the person.\n\nReturns the id it was stored under. `created: false` means an equivalent insight was already there and nothing was written — read `content`, and if yours says something different, send it again with `supersedes` set to that id. `related` carries live claims near the insight with their text, for the same decision.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "Kind": {
          "description": "What a memory *is*, which decides how it is used and how fast it fades.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule, always part of assembled context.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "derived_from": {
          "description": "The ids this insight was drawn from — memory ids, episode ids, or\nboth, exactly as `recall`, `remember` or `context` handed them to you.\nAt least one; an insight with nothing behind it belongs in `remember`.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "entities": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "The subjects it is about (\"user\", \"project-x\"), for filtered recall.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "insight": {
          "description": "The insight itself: one atomic, self-contained statement in the third\nperson, understandable with no conversation around it. What you\nconcluded, not the reasoning that got you there.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "kind": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "default": null,
          "description": "What the insight is; defaults to `lesson`."
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to store this. Defaults to the space this server was started\nwith; `user` is the reserved space for memory that follows the person\nacross every project.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "supersedes": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "The ids of the live claims this insight replaces. Each one is closed\nand stays readable and dated; only the insight is live afterwards.\n\nSeveral at once is a merge: one cited conclusion standing in for every\nuncited wording of it, closed in the same call rather than forgotten.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "tags": {
          "default": [],
          "description": "Free labels. `identity` marks a fact that belongs in every session's\nprofile.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "insight",
        "derived_from"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "Related": {
          "description": "A live claim near the insight that was just stored.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "What it says, so the two can be judged without a second lookup.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The id of the older claim — what to put in `supersedes`.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "similarity": {
              "description": "Cosine similarity between the two, below the duplicate threshold and\nabove the floor for being worth mentioning at all.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "content",
            "similarity"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "content": {
          "description": "What is stored under `id` — your wording when it was written, the\nexisting claim's when it was not.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "created": {
          "description": "Whether the row was written now.\n\n`false` means an equivalent insight was already stored and nothing\nchanged. Read `content`: if it says something other than what you sent,\nyours is a correction rather than a repetition, and it is still unsaid\nuntil you send it again with `id` in `supersedes`.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "derived_from": {
          "description": "The evidence recorded with the insight, as refs `inspect` takes as they\nstand.\n\nEmpty when `created` is false: nothing was written, and the claim\nalready under `id` keeps whatever evidence it was written with.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "id": {
          "description": "The insight's id — newly minted, or the id of the claim that already\nsaid this.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "note": {
          "description": "What to do about a write that did not happen, when there is something\nworth doing.\n\nIt appears when the insight was judged a duplicate of a claim that\n**carries no evidence of its own** — the conclusion is already stored,\nits provenance is not, and nothing here rewrites a stored claim. A\nno-op that leaves an uncited conclusion standing is not the same\noutcome as one that leaves a cited one standing, and only this says so.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "related": {
          "description": "Live claims near the insight, which it may be correcting.\n\nThese were **not** blocked and are not duplicates — they are here\nbecause a correction and the claim it corrects are near neighbours, and\nthis is the only moment the older one's id is in front of you. If one\nof them is what your insight replaces, send the insight again with its\nid in `supersedes` — several ids if several of them are the same claim\nworded differently. If it is merely related, ignore it.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Related"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "superseded": {
          "description": "The ids of the claims closed by a `supersedes` in this call.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "id",
        "created",
        "content",
        "derived_from",
        "related",
        "superseded"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "idempotentHint": true
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "remember",
    "description": "Store what this session learned — the distilled claims, and optionally the verbatim text they came from — so the next session starts with it instead of working it out again.\n\nCall it as soon as something durable is said, unprompted and without waiting for the end of the session: a preference or a standing instruction (\"always use X here\"), a decision and the reason behind it, a convention, a constraint, a lesson from something that failed. Nothing you say persists — an answer that ends \"noted\" or \"I'll remember that\" without a call to this tool is a promise the next session cannot keep.\n\nDistil before you call: one atomic, self-contained claim per entry, in the third person, understandable with no conversation around it — \"the user prefers Rust over Python for CLI tools\", not \"he said he likes it better\". Do not store what the code or the ticket already records, or what only matters to this turn.\n\nNothing here is ever rewritten. When something already stored turns out to be wrong, send the correction with `supersedes` set to the id of the claim it replaces, rather than storing a contradiction: the old claim stays readable and dated, and only one of them is live. You do not have to go looking for that id first — see `related` below.\n\nReturns a diff rather than an acknowledgement — what was created, what was already stored, what was closed, and the episode's id. Two of those carry the id and the text of a claim already in the store: `duplicates`, which were **not** written, and `related`, which sit alongside what was. Read the `content` of both before you answer. A correction reads much like the claim it corrects, so it lands in one of those lists rather than in `created` — and if you report it as remembered without checking, the claim that is still live is the old and wrong one. Neither list is a verdict: nothing here judges that two claims disagree, which is why they are handed back rather than acted on. When one of them says something your claim contradicts, send your claim again with `supersedes` set to its id.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "DecayClass": {
          "description": "How fast a memory's retention falls off between accesses.\n\nThe rates themselves live with the scoring functions; this is the label\nstored on the row.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "pinned",
              "description": "Never decays.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "slow",
              "description": "Fades over months.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "normal",
              "description": "The default: fades over weeks.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "fast",
              "description": "Working context: fades over days, then gets pruned at startup.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "EpisodeInput": {
          "description": "Verbatim ground truth to store alongside what was distilled from it.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "The text, unedited. It is chunked for retrieval but never rewritten.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "occurred_at": {
              "default": null,
              "description": "When the events described happened, RFC3339. Defaults to now.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            },
            "session": {
              "default": null,
              "description": "A grouping key for one conversation or working session.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "content"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "Kind": {
          "description": "What a memory *is*, which decides how it is used and how fast it fades.",
          "oneOf": [
            {
              "const": "fact",
              "description": "A distilled statement about the world, the user, or the project.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "lesson",
              "description": "A procedural insight: \"X fails when Y; do Z\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "const": "instruction",
              "description": "A standing behavioral rule, always part of assembled context.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          ]
        },
        "MemoryInput": {
          "description": "One claim to store.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "One atomic, self-contained statement in the third person — it has to\nstill make sense with no conversation around it. \"The user prefers Rust\nover Python for CLI tools\", not \"he said he likes it better\".",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "decay_class": {
              "anyOf": [
                {
                  "$ref": "#/$defs/DecayClass"
                },
                {
                  "type": "null"
                }
              ],
              "default": null,
              "description": "How fast this fades between uses. Defaults by kind: facts `normal`,\nlessons `slow`, instructions `pinned`."
            },
            "entities": {
              "default": [],
              "description": "The subjects this claim is about (\"user\", \"project-x\"), for filtered\nrecall.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "kind": {
              "anyOf": [
                {
                  "$ref": "#/$defs/Kind"
                },
                {
                  "type": "null"
                }
              ],
              "default": null,
              "description": "What the claim is; defaults to `fact`."
            },
            "supersedes": {
              "default": [],
              "description": "The ids of the live memories this claim replaces. Each one is closed\nand stays readable and dated; only this claim is live afterwards.\n\nOne id is a correction. Several is a merge — the wording worth keeping,\nclosing every duplicate of it in the same call, which is what\n`consolidate`'s `near_duplicates` clusters are for. Use this rather\nthan `forget` for anything that was once true: a closed claim keeps its\nhistory, a forgotten one does not.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "tags": {
              "default": [],
              "description": "Free labels. `identity` marks a fact that belongs in every session's\nprofile.",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            "valid_from": {
              "default": null,
              "description": "When the claim started being true, RFC3339. Defaults to now — set it\nwhen recording something that became true earlier.",
              "type": [
                "string",
                "null"
              ]
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "content"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "episode": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "$ref": "#/$defs/EpisodeInput"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "The verbatim text these claims came from, stored unedited as ground\ntruth. Every memory in the same call is provenanced to it."
        },
        "memories": {
          "description": "The distilled claims, one per entry. May be empty only when `episode`\nis present.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/MemoryInput"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "space": {
          "default": null,
          "description": "Where to store this. Defaults to the space this server was started\nwith; `user` is the reserved space for memory that follows the person\nacross every project.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "memories"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "outputSchema": {
      "$defs": {
        "Duplicate": {
          "description": "A claim that was already in the store.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "What the stored memory actually says.\n\nCompare it against what you sent: if it says something different, this\nis a claim you are correcting rather than one you are repeating, and it\nis still live until you re-send yours with `id` in `supersedes`.\nIn BM25-only mode this is your own text, which matched exactly once\ncase and whitespace were folded.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The id of the memory that already holds this claim.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "of": {
              "description": "Which entry of the `memories` you sent this refers to, zero-based.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "similarity": {
              "description": "How close the two are, as cosine similarity.\n\nText identical to what is stored reads a rounding error *short* of 1.0\n— 0.9999998 is typical — because verbatim input trips the vector gate\nas its own nearest neighbour before the content hash is ever consulted.\nExactly 1.0 means the hash matched with no embedding to compare it\nagainst, which is BM25-only mode.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "of",
            "content",
            "similarity"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        },
        "Related": {
          "description": "A live claim near one that was just stored.",
          "properties": {
            "content": {
              "description": "What the older memory says, so the contradiction can be judged without\na second lookup.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "id": {
              "description": "The id of the older memory — what to put in `supersedes`.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "of": {
              "description": "Which entry of the `memories` you sent this is a neighbour of,\nzero-based.",
              "format": "uint",
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            "similarity": {
              "description": "Cosine similarity between the two, below the duplicate threshold and\nabove the floor for being worth mentioning at all.",
              "format": "double",
              "type": "number"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "id",
            "of",
            "content",
            "similarity"
          ],
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "created": {
          "description": "Ids of the memories stored, in the order they were sent, skipping the\npositions reported in `duplicates`.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "duplicates": {
          "description": "The claims judged already stored. Nothing was written for these.\n\n**Read `content` before treating one as a no-op.** A correction is\nusually worded much like the claim it corrects, so it lands here too —\nand then nothing has changed, the older and now-wrong claim is what is\nstill live, and saying \"already noted\" would be false. If `content`\nsays something other than what you sent, re-send yours with the id\nhere in `supersedes`.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Duplicate"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "episode": {
          "description": "Id of the episode, whether it was written now or already stored.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "related": {
          "description": "Live claims about the same subject as something you just stored, which\nthe new claim may contradict.\n\nThese were **written** — they are not duplicates and nothing was\nblocked. They are here because a correction and the claim it corrects\nare near neighbours, and this is the only moment the id of the older\none is in front of you. If one of these is now wrong, re-send the new\nclaim with its id in `supersedes`: that closes the old one instead of\nleaving both live and contradicting each other. If several of them are\nthe same claim in different words, send every id — one call closes them\nall. If it is merely related, ignore it.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Related"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "superseded": {
          "description": "Ids of the memories closed by a `supersedes` in this call.",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "created",
        "duplicates",
        "related",
        "superseded"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "annotations": {
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "idempotentHint": true
    }
  }
]
